 "experiment": "EXP-054",
 "title": "Where an RTX 5090 MTP3 decode round spends its time, against the memory-bandwidth floor",
 "generated_utc": "2026-09-25T13:10:00Z",
 "status": "passed",
 "question": "Production decodes at 58.5 rounds/s (17.1 ms per round, 2.77 accepted tokens per round) with 99.4% of decode time waiting on the device. Which kernels hold the round above the floor that streaming its weights and KV at the measured 1,674.5 GB/s would set, and is a reachable gain of at least 5% of round time there?",
 "answer": "Yes. At a 26,000-token context the round takes 17.23 ms unprofiled, matching production's 17.1 ms, and the GPU is busy 99.2% of it, so launch overhead is not the gap. The kernels that stream weights at the floor are the W8 ones: the full LM head, the MTP layer and the Q4 draft-head GEMV run at 97-104% of it. The verify pass's Q4/Q5 projections do not: Q4 gate/up at 82.5%, Q5 down at 75.8%, the Q5 mixer outputs at 68.0%, GDN value/z at 66.2%, and the two small Q4 query/key projections at 39.7% (GDN) and 47.7% (attention). Bringing every mapped kernel to 90% of the floor recovers 2.6 ms per round (15.1%).",
 "decision": "Proceed to kernel work, largest reachable gain first, each change proved per kernel and end to end on the published profile. First: the verify pass's GDN input projection, which launches its Q4 query/key (4,096 rows) and Q5 value/z (12,288 rows) as two kernels over the same activations; one launch keeps each row's arithmetic and so its outputs bit-identical. Then the Q5 SIMT split-2 kernel (128 calls per round), then the attention input projections and attention decode.",
